  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalling cold front may bring rain at times\ntoday through Sunday.\n\nA prefrontal trough ahead of the main airmass change associated\nwith the approaching cold front has kicked off scattered\nshowers. These were most noticeable east of the Blue Ridge where\nslightly higher instability was present. This activity continues\nmoving south/east this evening, eventually exiting the area by\n11pm or so. Overrunning light showers linger, but not expecting\nmuch from these as dry surface air is now filtering into the \narea. Dew points have dropped to 60F at CBE and HGR as of 9PM,\nthough everywhere else has dew points around 70F. Conditions dry\nout for most overnight as the dry air pushes south. Not much of\na temperature change as lows settle in the mid 50s to low 60s.\n\nStrong high pressure will pass north of the area Saturday while\nthe front stalls just to our south. Southern stream moisture \nwill likely overrun the boundary fairly far to the north, but \nthere remains some uncertainty in the dividing line between rain\nand no rain. The trend has been for a further north track, so\nhave shifted rain chances about 20 to 30 miles north with the\nlatest forecast package. \n\nA wave of low pressure will ride along this stalled boundary \nSaturday night into Sunday, and this may provide a higher chance\nof some heavier rain spreading northward into the area. The\nWeather Prediction Center does have our eastern areas in a\nMarginal Excessive Rainfall Outlook, and this seems plausible\ngiven the potential for a surge of 2\"+ PWATs. Rain that does \noccur will likely exit the area Sunday evening.\n\nBeing north of the boundary and potentially having a lot of\ncloud cover, high temperatures will likely remain in the 60s for\nmost areas over the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...First widespread frost and freeze potential west of \nthe Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nA distinct pattern shift unfolds into the first full week of \nOctober. A highly amplified trough characterized by mid-level \nheights around 531-dm is expected to cross through Quebec and New \nBrunswick on Monday. While the core of the forcing remains across \nNew England and points northward, the main impact will come with the \ncold air arriving in the wake of this trough. A robust area of \nCanadian high pressure moves into the area Monday night through mid-\nweek. Consequently, this is forecast to bring a true autumnal feel \nto the air with daily temperatures well below average. For Tuesday \nand Wednesday, forecast highs will generally range from the upper \n50s to 60s, with mountain locales mainly in the 50s. Looking at \novernight conditions, the dry nature of the air mass allows \ntemperatures to plummet at night. Areas west of the Blue Ridge can \nexpect widespread mid/upper 30s on Monday and Tuesday nights. This \nwill also include some pockets of upper 20s to near the freezing \nmark. Given the growing season continues across the region, there \nwill be a threat of some frost/freeze conditions both of those \nnights. For those with sensitive outdoor vegetation, plans should be \nmade to ensure their safety in these conditions.\n\nAlthough Canadian high pressure moves off the coast toward the \nmid/late week period, the overall pattern will remain dry throughout \nnext week. Temperatures slowly rebound through the week as forecast \nhighs return to the low/mid 70s. Nighttime lows remain chilly, but \nthe threat for frost/freeze conditions should diminish beyond \nWednesday morning.",
